[
  {
    "text": "Human action, like a plucked string, vibrates and releases a pattern into the surrounding medium, producing effects that travel, resonate, decay, and return beyond the visible boundary of the action itself.",
    "topic": "Human Action as Vibrational Phenomenon"
  },
  {
    "text": "The metaphor of a plucked string is used to argue that human action is not self-contained but operates within a relational field of action, influencing and being influenced by its environment.",
    "topic": "Relational Field of Action"
  },
  {
    "text": "String theory provides the guiding metaphor for understanding human action as a dynamic, interconnected process rather than an isolated event.",
    "topic": "String Theory Metaphor"
  },
  {
    "text": "The paper suggests that recognizing human action as a vibrating entity corrects a persistent error in traditional understandings of action.",
    "topic": "Correction of Traditional Understanding"
  },
  {
    "text": "The concept of memory in human action is implied through the idea of reverberation and the return of effects beyond the initial action's boundary.",
    "topic": "Memory in Human Action"
  }
]
